

{"id":"cbbc7ca2-a792-4fc7-8389-0f9e502382b3","object":"chat.completion","created":1786450606,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_070\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_10\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 16,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_04\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 5,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_01\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 6,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_07\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 9,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_08\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 8,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_06\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_11\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 21,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task for a zero-shot repair. The task: produce one complete repair covering every mission exactly once. Respect fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and all intervention overrides. Optimize lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nThe given instance has baseline decisions that we need to modify according to interventions. The interventions include: initial_soc_overrides for UAV_03: initial_soc_kwh=2.7171 (instead of 3.809852), corridor closures: COR_06 closed from slot 8 to 31, COR_10 closed from slot 5 to 28. Pad capacity override: VP_01 pad_capacity at slot 5 is reduced to 1 (default is 2). No charger capacity overrides, no mission window overrides.\n\nWe must respect fixed UAV assignment and fixed UAV order. The missions have uav_id fixed: MISSION_01: UAV_02, MISSION_02: UAV_03, MISSION_03: UAV_02, MISSION_04: UAV_02, MISSION_05: UAV_03, MISSION_06: UAV_01. Also the order of missions per UAV is fixed by sequence_index. For UAV_02: MISSION_03 (seq 0), MISSION_04 (seq 1), MISSION_01 (seq 2). For UAV_03: MISSION_02 (seq 0), MISSION_05 (seq 1). For UAV_01: MISSION_06 (seq 0).\n\nWe must schedule each mission, choosing a path, depart_slot, and charge_blocks after the mission. We cannot hold any mission? The baseline held none. The intervention might cause infeasibility if we don't hold some missions. The optimization lexicographically: first minimize urgent holds, then total holds, then repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, energy. So we prefer not to hold urgent missions. Urgent missions are MISSION_02 (UAV_03) and MISSION_04 (UAV_02). We must try to not hold them. If we must hold, hold non-urgent first.\n\nWe need to compute feasibility given SOC and other constraints. Let's analyze each UAV's sequence.\n\nUAV_02: MISSION_03, then MISSION_04, then MISSION_01. UAV_02 initial SOC: 9.635464 kWh. Battery capacity 16 kWh. Reserve 1.5 kWh. Charging per block: 1.2 kWh per charge block. Max charge blocks 3. Start vertiport: VP_01. Turnaround slots: 1.\n\nUAV_03: MISSION_02, then MISSION_05. Initial SOC from override: 2.7171 kWh. Battery capacity 22 kWh. Reserve 2.5 kWh. Charge per block: 0.95 kWh. Max charge blocks 3. Start vertiport: VP_01. Turnaround: 1.\n\nUAV_01: MISSION_06 only. Initial SOC: 8.5 kWh. Battery capacity 22 kWh. Reserve 2.5 kWh. Charge per block: 0.95 kWh. Max 3. Start VP_01. Turnaround: 1.\n\nFirst, consider corridors closures: COR_06 closed from slot 8 to 31. COR_06 is used in PATH_06 for MISSION_04 (VP_05 to VP_03, travel slots 2, wind 0). Also COR_06 is used by PATH_06 which is a path option for MISSION_04. Corridor closures affect path availability: if a path uses a closed corridor during its slot, it's infeasible. So need to check path timing. Also COR_10 closed from slot 5 to 28. COR_10 used in PATH_09 for MISSION_02 (VP_01 to VP_05, travel slots 2, wind 0). PATH_09 is one of the options for MISSION_02.\n\nAlso pad capacity: VP_01 slot 5 capacity = 1 (pad). That may affect departures or landings at VP_01 at slot 5. Pad capacity is used for takeoff/landing. For each mission, it occupies origin pad at depart_slot, and destination pad at arrival slot (depart + travel). It might also use pad during turnaround? Typically, a UAV occupies pad at origin for departure slot, and at destination for arrival slot. If post-service charging uses pad? Not sure. Typically, pad capacity is for landing/takeoff. Charging might occur on pad if charging uses pad, but that's not explicitly stated. The task says \"pad_capacity_by_slot_default\" – likely pads are for vertiport landing/takeoff. The slot occupation: each mission uses origin pad at depart_slot and destination pad at arrival slot. Charging does not use pad capacity unless specified. The capacity constraints: must not exceed pad capacity at any slot.\n\nLet's list each mission's path options and energy consumption.\n\nMISSION_03 (UAV_02, seq 0): origin VP_01, dest VP_05. Path options: only PATH_01 (COR_05, distance 1.08 km, energy 0.135464 kWh, travel slots 2, wind 1). Release slot 5, due slot 28. So earliest depart is slot 5. Due slot 28 means arrival must be <= due slot? Typically due slot is latest arrival slot? The task uses due_slot, probably arrival must be on or before due_slot. Release_slot is earliest depart. So we need to choose a depart_slot such that depart >= release, and depart + travel_slots <= due? Actually due is likely latest arrival slot. So arrival_slot = depart_slot + travel_slots <= due_slot. So for PATH_01, travel 2 slots, so if depart at 5, arrival 7, well within due 28. Need to check energy and reserve.\n\nEnergy consumption: service flight energy = energy_kwh from path (includes empty vehicle energy and payload energy per km kg). That's already computed: 0.135464 kWh. Plus incoming deadhead? Incoming deadhead is null for all missions in this instance. So no incoming deadhead energy. Then reserve check: after service flight, SOC must be >= reserve (1.5). So initial SOC - service energy >= reserve? Actually order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> turnaround -> post-service charge. So we start with initial SOC (or after previous charge). Then incoming deadhead energy is deducted. Then service flight energy deducted. Then check reserve: SOC after these must be >= reserve_kwh. If not, need to charge earlier? Or we can charge only at post-service? Actually, charging is only post-service after turnaround. So we need to have enough SOC before mission to cover incoming deadhead (if any) + service flight, and still have at least reserve after that. Then after turnaround, we charge. So for first mission, we start with initial SOC. For MISSION_03, no incoming deadhead, so energy needed = service energy (0.135464). After that, SOC = initial 9.635464 - 0.135464 = 9.5 kWh, well above reserve 1.5. Then turnaround 1 slot, then post-service charge. We can choose charge_blocks (0-3). Charging adds charge_kwh_per_block * min(charge_blocks, remaining empty capacity). Battery capacity 16 kWh, so max charge space. So we could charge 3 blocks: 3*1.2 = 3.6 kWh, SOC would be 9.5 + 3.6 = 13.1, but capped at 16, so 13.1. So feasible. Depart slot must be at least release slot 5, and must respect pad capacity at VP_01 at slot 5. Pad VP_01 slot 5 capacity is overridden to 1. So if both MISSION_03 and MISSION_02 (UAV_03) depart at slot 5 from VP_01, that would be 2 UAVs needing pad, but capacity is 1. So conflict. So at most one of them can depart at slot 5. Also MISSION_06 (UAV_01) release slot 21, but that's later. So we need to decide which UAV departs when. Also MISSION_02 (UAV_03) release slot 5, but origin VP_01. So both MISSION_03 and MISSION_02 want to depart from VP_01 at slot 5 or later. Also MISSION_03 due slot 28 is generous, so it can be delayed if needed. MISSION_02 is urgent, due slot 7, so must arrive by slot 7. Travel slots for MISSION_02: path options with travel slots 2 or 3 (or 2 for PATH_09 but corridor closed). Let's examine MISSION_02.\n\nMISSION_02: UAV_03, seq 0. Origin VP_01, dest VP_05. Release slot 5, due slot 7. Path options:\n- PATH_04: corridor COR_02, distance 0.9, energy 0.144733, travel slots 2, wind 1.\n- PATH_05: corridor COR_03, travel slots 3, wind 1, energy 0.160815.\n- PATH_09: corridor COR_10, travel slots 2, wind 0, energy 0.1812. BUT COR_10 is closed from slot 5 to 28. If we depart at slot 5, travel slots 2, corridor usage at slot 5 and 6? Actually edge_segments: \"edge_segments\":[{\"corridor_id\":\"COR_10\",\"duration_slots\":2,\"offset_slot\":0,\"wind_level\":0}]. The corridor is used from depart_slot+offset for duration. So if depart_slot=5, COR_10 used at slot 5 and 6. Both closed (5-6 are in closed range 5-28). So PATH_09 infeasible at any depart slot before 28? Wait, closings slots 5-28 inclusive. If we depart at 29, travel slots 2, using COR_10 at 29 and 30, those are not closed (closed up to 28). So PATH_09 could be used for depart_slot >= 29. But due slot is 7, so impossible. Thus PATH_09 is infeasible for this mission.\n\nSo feasible paths are PATH_04 (2 slots) or PATH_05 (3 slots). To arrive by due slot 7, with travel 2 slots: depart at 5 -> arrival 7; depart at 6 -> arrival 8 (late). So only depart 5 with PATH_04 meets due. With PATH_05 (3 slots), earliest arrival 8, too late. So ONLY PATH_04 with depart_slot=5 works. So MISSION_02 MUST depart at slot 5 from VP_01 using PATH_04. That means pad VP_01 at slot 5 is used by UAV_03 for MISSION_02 departure. Pad capacity VP_01 slot 5 is 1. So no other UAV can depart from VP_01 at slot 5. So MISSION_03 (UAV_02) cannot depart at slot 5. It can depart at slot 6 or later. Also MISSION_06 (UAV_01) release 21, so later.\n\nNow check MISSION_03: release 5, due 28. PATH_01 travel 2 slots. So if depart at 6, arrival 8, okay. Energy needed: 0.135464, initial SOC 9.635464, okay. So we can schedule MISSION_03 depart 6. But need to check pad capacity at VP_01 at slot 6: default capacity 2, no override, so fine. Also check corridor COR_05 at slots 6-7 (2 slots). No closure, default capacity 2, so fine. So MISSION_03 can go at depart 6.\n\nBut wait: UAV_02 has sequence: MISSION_03, then MISSION_04, then MISSION_01. After MISSION_03 arrival at slot 8 (if depart 6, travel 2: 6+2=8). Then turnaround 1 slot at destination VP_05. Turnaround means the UAV is at VP_05, probably occupying pad? Usually turnaround is time at destination before charging? The order: service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So after arrival, there is turnaround time (1 slot) at destination, then charge blocks. During turnaround, the UAV is likely at a pad? Possibly pad occupancy for landing and turnaround. But capacity: pad at VP_05 for slot 8: MISSION_03 arrives at 8, need a pad for landing. Then turnaround slot 9 might also occupy that pad? Typically, the UAV occupies pad from arrival through turnaround and charging? In many UAM problems, the pad is occupied from landing until takeoff. But here charge blocks are post-service charge, which may occur on a charger, not necessarily pad. The pad capacity is separate from charger capacity. The task says: \"charger capacity applies to every occupied post-service charging slot at the destination.\" It doesn't say pad capacity applies to charging. So I'll assume pad is only for landing and takeoff, and charging uses charger but not pad. So at destination, UAV needs pad for the arrival slot only (the landing). During turnaround slot, maybe it's still on pad? But if it's not taking off immediately, it might be moved to a parking area. Without specific, I'll assume pad occupancy only for the departure slot at origin and arrival slot at destination. But need to consider if pad is needed for turnaround. The baseline decisions: e.g., MISSION_04 in baseline: depart 8, path PATH_06 travel 2, arrival 10. That's urgent, due 11. So arrival 10, then turnaround 1 slot at VP_03, then maybe charge. We'll see. I'll assume pad occupancy only for the exact slot of departure (origin) and arrival (destination). So MISSION_03 arrival at VP_05 slot 8 uses a pad at VP_05 slot 8. That's fine, capacity 2. No overrides.\n\nThen MISSION_03 can charge after turnaround. We can choose charge_blocks. After MISSION_03 service flight, SOC is initial 9.635464 - 0.135464 = 9.5 kWh. After turnaround, we charge. Since MISSION_04 is next for UAV_02, which departs from VP_05 (the destination of MISSION_03). MISSION_04 origin is VP_05, and it needs to go to VP_03. MISSION_04 release slot 8, due 11, urgent. UAV_02 after MISSION_03 will be at VP_05 with some SOC. MISSION_04 has path options: PATH_03 (4 slots, wind 0, energy 0.226), PATH_06 (2 slots, wind 0, energy 0.113), PATH_07 (1 slot, wind 1, energy 0.12543). However, COR_06 (used in PATH_06) is closed from slot 8 onward. So PATH_06 is infeasible if it uses COR_06 during closed slots. Let's check PATH_06: edge_segments: corridor_id COR_06, duration 2 slots, offset 0. So if depart slot is D, uses COR_06 in slots D and D+1. Since COR_06 closed from slot 8 to 31, if D>=8, then D and D+1 will be >=8, so closed. If D=7, D=7, D+1=8: slot 7 not closed? Closure starts at slot 8. So slot 7 is open, slot 8 is closed. So if we depart at 7, travel slots 2: uses slots 7 and 8? Edge segment duration 2, offset 0, so it uses corridor at depart_slot and depart_slot+1. So if depart 7, uses slots 7,8. Slot 8 closed, so infeasible. If depart 6, uses 6,7: slot 6 open, slot 7 open, but depart 6 from VP_05 would mean UAV_02 arrives at VP_05 from MISSION_03 at slot 8, so it can't depart earlier. So any feasible departure for MISSION_04 will be at least slot 8 after turnaround of MISSION_03. MISSION_03 arrival 8, turnaround 1 slot = slot 9? Actually order: service flight arrives at D (if depart 6, arrival 8). Then reserve check, then turnaround (1 slot). Turnaround happens after arrival. So the next mission can depart at earliest slot: arrival_slot + turnaround. So if MISSION_03 arrival 8, turnaround 1 means the UAV is free to depart next mission at slot 9. So earliest depart for MISSION_04 is 9. So PATH_06 would use slots 9 and 10, both closed. So PATH_06 impossible. PATH_03: 4 slots, uses COR_04, which is not closed. Check COR_04: no closure, capacity 2. So PATH_03 feasible. Energy 0.226 kWh. PATH_07: 1 slot, uses COR_01 (wind 1). COR_01 not closed, capacity 2. Energy 0.12543 kWh. So both PATH_03 and PATH_07 are possible. PATH_07 travel 1 slot, energy lower. So optimal likely PATH_07.\n\nNow check due slot 11. Depart at 9 with PATH_07: arrival 10 (9+1=10) <= due 11. So okay. Depart at 9 with PATH_03: arrival 13 (9+4=13) >11, late. So PATH_07 is only feasible to meet due. So MISSION_04 must depart at 9 or earlier? But earliest is 9. So depart 9 PATH_07.\n\nNow energy: MISSION_04 needs service energy 0.12543 kWh. We must ensure UAV_02's SOC before MISSION_04 is enough to cover incoming deadhead (none) + service energy + reserve. So before MISSION_04, the SOC = SOC after MISSION_03 turnaround and charging. So we need to charge after MISSION_03 enough so that SOC_at_depart_MISSION_04 - service_energy >= reserve. Reserve is 1.5. So need SOC before departure >= 0.12543 + 1.5 = 1.62543 kWh. That's easy. But we also need to ensure we don't exceed battery capacity. After MISSION_03, SOC is 9.5 (arrival). Then turnaround, then charge. We can choose charge_blocks. If we charge, SOC increases. After charging, we have SOC for next mission. Also we may need energy for upcoming MISSION_01 later. So we might charge more.\n\nMISSION_01: UAV_02, seq 2. Origin VP_03, dest VP_06. Release 16, due 25. After MISSION_04, UAV_02 arrives at VP_03 at slot 10 (depart 9 +1 =10). Turnaround 1 slot, so ready for next mission at slot 11. But release of MISSION_01 is 16, so there is a gap. UAV_02 can charge at VP_03 after MISSION_04. But it must also consider pad capacity at VP_01 for MISSION_01? No, MISSION_01 origin VP_03, so no conflict. MISSION_01 path options: only PATH_10 (COR_08, distance 1.95, energy 0.244589, travel 1 slot, wind 1). So travel 1 slot. So if depart at 16, arrival 17, due 25. So feasible.\n\nNow energy: SOC after MISSION_04: initial SOC for MISSION_04 is SOC after MISSION_03 charging minus MISSION_04 service energy. Let's calculate. Let x = charge_blocks on MISSION_03. After MISSION_03, SOC before charging = 9.5. Then charge x blocks: max increase = x*1.2, but cannot exceed 16. So SOC after charge = min(9.5 + 1.2x, 16). Then MISSION_04 departs at slot 9, consumes 0.12543, so arrival SOC = SOC_after_charge - 0.12543. Then turnaround 1 slot, then charge at VP_03 before MISSION_01. We can also set charge_blocks for MISSION_04. After charging, SOC must be enough for MISSION_01: need >= service_energy (0.244589) + reserve (1.5) = 1.744589 kWh. So any charging will easily meet this. Could even not charge and still have >9.5-0.12543 = ~9.374, far above. So we don't need much charging. But we might want to optimize energy: the final objective includes energy. So we want to minimize energy used? Actually \"energy\" as last objective: we want to minimize total energy consumption? Or minimize something about energy. Usually it's minimize energy used for flights plus maybe charging? The lexicographic: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy. Energy could be total energy consumed (flight energy). So we want to minimize flight energy by choosing paths? Or maybe it's minimize total energy (including charging)? The description: \"delay plus charge wait, then energy.\" Likely energy refers to the total energy expended (flight energy) or maybe the sum of energy consumed. Since we have to choose paths, we can affect flight energy. So after ensuring no holds, we want smaller energy. But given that we need to satisfy due dates, we may choose the path with minimum energy for each mission. For MISSION_04, PATH_07 has 0.12543, PATH_03 has 0.226, so PATH_07 is lower energy and feasible. For MISSION_03, there's only PATH_01, energy 0.135464. For MISSION_01, only PATH_10, energy 0.244589. So flight energy is fixed for those. For MISSION_02, we must choose PATH_04 (0.144733) or PATH_05 (0.160815). PATH_04 is lower energy and meets due, so choose PATH_04. For MISSION_05 and MISSION_06 we have choices.\n\nNow UAV_03: MISSION_02 (urgent) depart 5, path PATH_04, arrive 7. Turnaround 1, ready at slot 8. Then MISSION_05: origin VP_05, dest VP_02, release 8, due 23. Path options: only PATH_08 (COR_09, distance 0.8, energy 0.128652, travel 1 slot, wind 1). So if depart at 8, arrival 9, within due. But need to check if pad at VP_05 is available at slot 8? MISSION_02 arrives at VP_05 slot 7. MISSION_05 departs at 8 from VP_05. Pad at VP_05: capacity 2. So no conflict. Also UAV_03 initial SOC override: 2.7171 kWh. Battery capacity 22, reserve 2.5.\n\nEnergy for MISSION_02: service energy 0.144733. Starting SOC 2.7171. After service: 2.7171 - 0.144733 = 2.572367. Reserve 2.5, so 2.572367 >= 2.5, just barely meets reserve. So MISSION_02 can complete without pre-charge. After arrival, turnaround, then post-service charge. We can charge. Then MISSION_05 needs 0.128652 + reserve 2.5 = 2.628652. So SOC before MISSION_05 must be at least 2.628652. After MISSION_02 arrival, SOC = 2.572367. If we don't charge, SOC is 2.572367 < 2.628652, not enough. So we must charge at least enough to raise SOC by (2.628652 - 2.572367) = 0.056285 kWh. Charge per block is 0.95 kWh. So even 1 block gives 0.95, SOC becomes min(2.572367+0.95, 22) = 3.522367, sufficient. So we need at least 1 charge block after MISSION_02. So charge_blocks for MISSION_02 must be >=1. We can do 1,2,3.\n\nThen after MISSION_05, UAV_03 has no more missions, so we don't need to charge. So MISSION_05 charge_blocks can be 0.\n\nNow UAV_01: MISSION_06, release 21, due 28. Origin VP_01, dest VP_04. Path options: PATH_02 (via COR_07 and COR_12, distance 1.24, energy 0.193325, travel 2 slots, wind 0/1) and PATH_11 (COR_11, distance 1.0, energy 0.151, travel 2 slots, wind 0). Both have travel 2 slots. Need to check corridor closures: COR_07 and COR_12 are not closed. COR_11 not closed. So both feasible. PATH_11 has lower energy (0.151 vs 0.193325). So we'd prefer PATH_11. Depart slot: earliest 21, arrival 23, due 28. So depart 21 works. Pad VP_01 at slot 21: capacity 2, no override. So feasible. Energy: initial SOC 8.5. Service energy 0.151 (if PATH_11). After service: 8.5 - 0.151 = 8.349. Reserve 2.5, ok. Turnaround at VP_04, then charge optional. No following missions, so charge_blocks=0.\n\nBut we must also consider that MISSION_06 is the only mission for UAV_01, so that's fine.\n\nNow check for any conflicts: pad capacity at VP_01 slot 5: already used by MISSION_02. So MISSION_03 must depart at slot 6 or later. Slot 6 VP_01 pad capacity 2, no override, so okay. MISSION_03 depart 6, arrival 8. Then MISSION_04 depart 9 from VP_05. Are there any pad conflicts at VP_05? MISSION_02 arrives at 7, uses pad at VP_05 slot 7. MISSION_03 arrives at 8, uses pad at VP_05 slot 8. MISSION_05 departs from VP_05 at slot 8? Actually MISSION_05 release 8, but it can depart at 8 if UAV_03 ready at 8 (MISSION_02 turnaround 1, so ready at 8). Depart at 8 uses pad at VP_05 slot 8 as well (departure). So at slot 8, we have MISSION_03 landing and MISSION_05 taking off. That's 2 operations at VP_05 slot 8. Pad capacity at VP_05 is 2, so okay. No conflict. Also MISSION_04 departure from VP_05 at 9, uses pad slot 9, no conflict.\n\nNow check corridor capacities: PATH_04 uses COR_02 at slot 5 and 6 (if depart 5, travel 2). Capacity 2, only one user, fine. PATH_01 uses COR_05 at slots 6,7 (depart 6). Capacity 2, fine. PATH_07 uses COR_01 at slot 9 (depart 9). COR_01 capacity 2, fine. PATH_08 uses COR_09 at slot 8 (depart 8). Capacity 2, fine. PATH_10 uses COR_08 at slot 16 (depart 16). Capacity 2, fine. PATH_11 uses COR_11 at slots 21,22 (depart 21). Capacity 2, fine. So no corridor capacity issues.\n\nNow charging: After MISSION_02, charge_blocks >=1. After MISSION_03, we can set charge_blocks. But need to ensure that charging does not exceed charger capacity at destination. Charger capacity default is 2 per vertiport. So at VP_05, after MISSION_02, charging slots? Post-service charge occurs after turnaround. For MISSION_02 arrival 7, turnaround 1 slot (slot 8?), Actually order: service flight arrival slot A, reserve check (instant), then turnaround 1 slot. So turnaround occupies slot A+1? Usually turnaround takes a slot. Post-service charge occurs after turnaround, i.e., starting at slot A+1+? The exact event order: \"incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". So it seems turnaround is a duration of 1 slot at the destination. So after arrival, the UAV goes through turnaround for 1 slot, then begins charging. So the post-service charge blocks start after the turnaround slot. So if arrival at slot 7, turnaround occupies slot 8 (the next slot). Then charge blocks start at slot 9 and take consecutive slots. So for charge_blocks = x, charging occupies slots 9,10,...,9+x-1. So charger occupation at VP_05 for MISSION_02: if x=1, slot 9; if x=2, slots 9-10; if x=3, slots 9-11. Similarly, for MISSION_03 arrival 8, turnaround slot 9, charge starts slot 10. So if MISSION_03 charges, it would occupy slots 10,11,... overlapping with possible MISSION_02's charging if MISSION_02 uses 2 or 3 blocks. So need to check charger capacity at VP_05 (2). So if both charge simultaneously, total chargers used at each slot must be <=2. So we need to design charge_blocks of MISSION_02 and MISSION_03 to avoid exceeding 2. Also for MISSION_04 at VP_03: arrival 10, turnaround slot 11, charge starts slot 12. Only one UAV there (UAV_02). No other missions at VP_03, so capacity 2 fine.\n\nAlso MISSION_05 arrival VP_02 slot 9 (depart 8+1=9). Turnaround 1 slot 10, then charging (if any) starts 11. No other at VP_02, fine.\n\nMISSION_01 arrival VP_06 slot 17 (depart 16+1=17). Turnaround 18, charge starts 19. VP_06 no other, fine.\n\nMISSION_06 arrival VP_04 slot 23 (depart 21+2=23), turnaround 24, charge starts 25. VP_04 no other, fine.\n\nSo the only potential contention is charging at VP_05 between MISSION_02 (UAV_03) and MISSION_03 (UAV_02). MISSION_02 charging occupying slots starting at 9. MISSION_03 charging starting at 10. So slots: if MISSION_02 x=1: occupies slot 9 only. MISSION_03 y=0: no charge. So no overlap. If MISSION_02 x=2: occupies 9,10. MISSION_03 if y>=1, occupies 10,... So slot 10 would have both, requiring 2 chargers, okay capacity 2. If x=3: occupies 9,10,11. MISSION_03 y=1: slot 10; y=2: 10-11; y=3: 10-12. So at slots 10 and 11, both could be using. Since capacity is 2, it's fine as long as total <=2. With x=3, MISSION_02 occupies 9,10,11. MISSION_03 if y=1 occupies 10 only: at 10 total 2. If y=2: 10,11: total 2 each. If y=3: 10,11,12: at 10,11 total 2 each, at 12 only 1. So all within capacity 2. So no violation as long as we do not have both using more than 2 chargers. But note: each UAV uses 1 charger per slot, so max 2. So as long as we don't have three UAVs charging at that vertiport simultaneously, it's fine. So any combination is okay.\n\nBut we might want to minimize total charge wait (part of delay plus charge wait). The objective i